/* Methods and standards (BACKLOG idea 5a, 18 November 2026): the page that tells a researcher how to read everything the app says (src/js/2z-methods-core.js, 2z-data-methods.js, 6zze-methods.js).
   Nine parts, each a one-line answer and the detail behind a disclosure. What this test holds, because a page of this kind is only worth having if every sentence on it is true:
   1. THE SHAPE: nine parts in the order of the brief, each with a title, a line and a disclosure; a contents list; one part opened by its address; every tag closed.
   2. THE COUNTS EQUAL THE LIVE DATA: the Library, the Atlas, the History timeline and Conflicts and peace as the page counts them are what the data says; the kinds of text and the grades of the sources too.
   3. THE LABELS ARE THE DATA’S: the three statuses, the three grades (and that no source has another), the date confidences, the grades of High-control groups; each example is a real entry with the status it is shown for.
   4. THE LOG AND THE STAGES MATCH THE DATA FILES: wherever a data file holds a number the log or the table gives (86 and 207 events, 34 of 179 rows, 13 cards, 6 bodies and 6 books, 29 sources …), they are equal.
   5. THE RELEASE NOTES: stamps are real, in order, and the current one is listed or pending.
   6. THE CITATIONS: Chicago and BibTeX for the app (with the stamp, or saying there is none), a text and an event; dates; braces balanced.
   7. EVERY LINK RESOLVES: each address on the page opens a page the app has; the example addresses name real entries; the page has no outside link.
   8. NO LOADED WORDS and no website name the Library keeps no name of.
   9. THE CLAIMS TIE TO THE CODE: the privacy sentences to tests/privacy.test.js and to the Setup code (what Export and Delete everything do), Ask an AI, the note for the maker only while the beta switch is on, the
      “Which translation?” cards not yet in the reader, the footer and Setup links, the palette words.
   Where a built folder is not there (atlas/, texts/) the checks that need it are skipped, as other tests do. Run: node tests/methods.test.js */
